# Master pre-pilot resolution (all recommendations: PROPOSED — NOT ADOPTED)

Prepared 2026-10-13. Static reading of the sources plus three in-memory computations with the existing code (the SF case matrix, prompt sizes from `prompt_table.json`, and an interrupt simulation with a mock provider in a temporary folder). No protocol, code, record, manifest, registry or historical result was changed; no live call, pilot or main run was made; the pilot gate is closed. Existing documents are referenced, not repeated: `CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_BRIEF.md` (sections 1-4 in depth), `PROPOSED_FINAL_DECISIONS.md`, `PROVIDER_RESPONSE_VALIDATION_REPORT.md`, `PRE_PILOT_FIX_REPORT.md`, `ADVISOR_DECISION_BRIEF.md`. Companion files: `MASTER_PROPOSED_DECISION_RECORD.md`, `MASTER_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`, `MASTER_PILOT_READINESS_CHECKLIST.md`, `MASTER_CONFLICT_INDEX.md` (conflict IDs C-01 to C-30 are defined there).

Paths: spec = `research_design/SCENARIO_CONSTRUCTION_SPECIFICATION_v2_7_DRAFT.md`; record = `research_design/STEP3_OWNER_DECISIONS_RECORD_DRAFT.md`. Labels: `CURRENT RULE`, `CONFLICT`, `RECOMMENDATION`, `OWNER/ADVISOR DECISION` (always PENDING here), `IMPLEMENTATION PLAN` (only after adoption; see the plan file).

## 1. Executive summary
1. **Pilot cannot open yet** for human reasons (no blind check, no evidence file, no valid authorization) and for rule reasons below.
2. **Most urgent rule conflict: C-04.** The pilot decision is computed from one "primary" SF type per call, but spec §13.6 is written on the SF definitions a call meets. A call that meets SF1 or SF4 together with SF3 or SF5 is counted as soft and can PASS (confirmed by running the real code: section 2). Real adapters do not produce these records today, but the code permits them and no test covers them.
3. **Rule gaps that bite before the first pilot call** because FZ is hashed before it (spec §13.2 line 797): the FZ hash object (C-03), the codebook (kappa, re-code values: C-02, C-09), the PG11b scan (C-07), the §18.2 policy (C-08), the timeout meaning (C-16). The runner enforces none of the FZ items except through the Step 7 verifier; it never mentions an FZ hash.
4. **Cost is not the binding constraint.** The US$100 limit is reached only at about 63,000 input tokens per call; actual prompts are at most 1,016 characters. The open point in OD-10 is the stated basis of the input size, not the budget (C-01).
5. **Resume (C-17)** cannot be built from the text; a local simulation shows what an interrupt leaves behind (section 5).
6. Everything proposed below needs either a record entry, a new methodology version, a code patch after adoption, or a human task; the classes are in the matrix of section 10.

## 2. SF flags, scoring and the pilot decision (C-04, C-05)
**CURRENT RULE.** Spec §13.4 (lines 807-816) defines SF1 transport/HTTP/API error or timeout, SF2 no non-whitespace character, SF3 provider finish reason at the output-token limit, SF4 safety/blocked finish reason or no candidate, SF5 harness exception on a non-empty response; "Structural failures are SF1-SF5". §13.6 (lines 828-834): PASS needs "no SF2, no SF3, no SF5; `SF1 + SF4 <= f_S`; UNP share <= theta_unp"; FAIL-S is "any SF2, SF3, SF5, or `SF1 + SF4 > f_S`". §13.7 (line 869): the pilot reports "SF1-SF5 counts, UNP share, latency and token usage". Record 12.18 row 1 (line 506): the SF and UNP counts are disjoint. Record 12.36 (line 793): fail-closed additions (error marker or non-2xx status ⇒ SF1; prompt block reason ⇒ SF4). FZ-v1 row 18: `f_S` = 1; PJR J-10: "tolerates one transport or block failure in 18 calls". Code: `step10/s10_scoring.py` `sf_flags` (46-63) returns all flags in `PRIMARY_ORDER = (SF1, SF4, SF3, SF2, SF5)` (line 20); `classify` takes the first as `primary_sf`; `pilot_counts` increments one counter per call (line 92); `step10/s11_pilot.py` `report` (34-40) passes those counts to `step7/s7_decision.py` `round_outcome` (lines 75-80: `hard = SF2+SF3+SF5`, `soft = SF1+SF4`, `fail_s = hard > 0 or soft > f_S`).

**Three concepts.** (1) all flags detected on one response: `sf_flags`, stored in the ledger `classified` event; (2) primary classification for the record: the first flag in `PRIMARY_ORDER` — an order that appears in no source text (12.30; Policy 2 package §4.5); (3) the counts that decide PASS/FAIL-S/FAIL-U/STOP: currently the primary-type vector, not the flag sets.

**Case matrix (computed with the real code; "pilot" = one such call plus 17 clean calls, `f_S` = 1, theta 0.50).**
| Case (record) | All flags | Primary (current) | Pilot outcome now | Outcome required by the text of §13.6 | False pass / false fail | Pilot result can change? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| SF1 only (503, no text) | SF1 | SF1 | PASS | PASS (soft 1 <= 1) | none | no |
| SF2 only (200, empty) | SF2 | SF2 | FAIL-S | FAIL-S | none | no |
| SF3 only (MAX_TOKENS, verdict present) | SF3 | SF3 | FAIL-S | FAIL-S | none | no |
| SF4 only (SAFETY, or no candidate, or prompt block) | SF4 | SF4 | PASS | PASS | none | no |
| SF5 only (parser raised) | SF5 | SF5 | FAIL-S | FAIL-S | none | no |
| SF3 + SF2 (MAX_TOKENS, empty) | SF3, SF2 | SF3 | FAIL-S | FAIL-S | none | no |
| SF1 + SF3 (error marker, text, MAX_TOKENS) | SF1, SF3 | SF1 | **PASS** | **FAIL-S** (SF3 met) | **false pass** | yes |
| SF1 + SF5 | SF1, SF5 | SF1 | **PASS** | **FAIL-S** | **false pass** | yes |
| SF4 + SF3 (prompt block + MAX_TOKENS) | SF4, SF3 | SF4 | **PASS** | **FAIL-S** | **false pass** | yes |
| SF4 + SF5 | SF4, SF5 | SF4 | **PASS** | **FAIL-S** | **false pass** | yes |
| SF1 + SF4 | SF1, SF4 | SF1 | PASS | PASS if `f_S` counts calls; FAIL-S if it counts flags (1+1 = 2 > 1) | none or false pass, depends on the unit | yes, with the unit |
| SF1 + SF3 + SF5 | SF1, SF3, SF5 | SF1 | **PASS** | **FAIL-S** | **false pass** | yes |
| SF4 + SF3 + SF5 | SF4, SF3, SF5 | SF4 | **PASS** | **FAIL-S** | **false pass** | yes |
| SF3 + SF5 | SF3, SF5 | SF3 | FAIL-S | FAIL-S | none | no |
There is no false-fail case in this matrix: the current construction can only under-count hard failures. With two such calls the soft count 2 > `f_S` would give FAIL-S; one is enough to pass wrongly.
**Reachability (UNRESOLVED for live payloads).** The adapter as written returns text "" and no finish reason on HTTP errors and exceptions (SF1 only), and no error marker on a 200. The false-pass rows need a payload with candidates plus a prompt block, or an error marker plus a finish reason; whether the live API can produce them is a live fact. `test_provider_response.py` covers the single-flag rows only.

**CONFLICT.** §13.6 is written on definitions met; 12.18 row 1 asks the counts to be disjoint; a disjoint vector needs a choice of which count a multi-flag call enters; that choice is the unapproved primary order. Also unresolved: whether `SF1 + SF4` adds flags or calls (the text adds counts; PJR J-10 reads calls).

**OPTIONS.** S1 keep (false passes remain); S2 hard-first primary order (SF3, SF2, SF5, then SF1, SF4) with the vector and `round_outcome` unchanged; S3 compute `hard_calls` (any of SF2/SF3/SF5) and `soft_calls` (other SF calls) from the flag sets and pass them as the counts, keep the order only for the per-type table; S4 count soft per flag; S5 stop for owner review on any multi-flag call.
**RECOMMENDATION.** S3, soft counted per call, plus S5 for a call with a hard flag together with SF1/SF4 until a live example has been seen. Class: RECORD ONLY (counting convention) + CODE; no protocol change. The primary order must not be treated as the decision rule. **Acceptance criteria (derive from the adopted rule, not from the code):** for every row of the matrix above plus all two- and three-flag combinations, the outcome equals the outcome computed by hand from §13.6 under the adopted unit; no combination containing SF2, SF3 or SF5 can PASS; single-flag rows unchanged. **OWNER/ADVISOR DECISION: PENDING.** Code is not to be changed before it.

## 3. Decisions D1-D7 and Q-P1 to Q-P4
For each: **Rule** (source), **State** (implementation), **Conflict**, **Options**, **Recommendation** (PROPOSED — NOT ADOPTED), **Consequences/Impact** (code, data, analysis, cost, reporting), **Protocol change?**, **Decision needed**, **Acceptance**.

**D1 PG3 (C-06).** Rule: spec §8.1 PG3 (line 461) "the set of differing character positions is contiguous and inside the target token's span"; §8.2 (line 525) "a single changed span". State: `step8/s8_gates.py` enforces "inside the token span" and counts strict non-consecutive pairs (180 in main; 8 of 8 pilot MIL templates are affected as well); Implementation B uses the same reading. Conflict: strict reading rejects 90 of 104 MIL templates (shared decimal point). Options: ratify span reading; strict reading (build rejected, steps 3-9 repeat); amend text. Recommendation: ratify. Impact: none on artefacts if ratified; large cost if strict. Protocol change: no for ratification (clarification), yes for an amendment. Decision: owner/advisor. Acceptance: record entry; `gate_report.json` unchanged; if strict is chosen, a rejected build and a new parameter set.

**D2 PG11b (C-07).** Rule: spec §8.2 (lines 519-520) "no pattern PT1, PT3-PT8b occurs in any file name, file body or directory name"; application after the run (line 520 (ii); §21.1 gate-stage rule line 1195). State: `step7/s7_bundle.py` lines 30, 64-98 scan response text; directory check only on the last path component. Conflict: ordinary words ("CLEAR", "PAC", "(valid)", "class = A") are rejected (synthetic test, `CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_BRIEF.md`); rebuilding cannot help (§21.2 row 6). Options: keep; narrow scope; machine-form only; mask. Recommendation: keep for the pilot and first run, state the risk, decide any narrowing before the FZ hash. Impact: secondary S2/S3 coding only. Protocol change: any narrowing yes (new methodology version, builders are FZ items, record 12.14). Acceptance if kept: record entry naming the risk; if narrowed: amended §8.2 text, Step 7 re-freeze, new tests.

**D3 Structural failures and UNP (C-08).** Rule: spec §13.4, §13.6, §13.8 (871-875), §18.2 (1051-1058: four proportions over `n_calls`, "unclear and unparseable stay in the denominator", sum to 1), §22 item 6 (1223); 12.18 row 1; 12.26 item 1. State: pilot SF-first; main interim reporting in `s10_analysis.py` (SF kept in `n_calls`, reported beside, no rescaling); `retries = 0` so no failed call is replaced. Conflict: "sum to 1" cannot hold with an SF call. Options: interim (v2.7 as written), Policy 1, Policy 2, parse-first. Recommendation: interim now; Policy 2 only if the advisor wants a sum-to-1 statement. Impact: nothing hidden under the interim rule; failures stay visible and in the denominator; Policy 1/2 change denominators or classes. Protocol change: interim no; others yes. Acceptance (interim): `RESULTS.md` shows SF counts and the statement that the four proportions sum to 1 minus the SF rate.

**D4 Flags and primary classification (C-04).** See section 2. Protocol change: no. Acceptance: matrix criteria.

**D5 Re-code and kappa (C-02, C-09).** Rule: spec §16.3 lines 1000-1003 (columns `coder1_*`/`coder2_*`; "Agreement is reported per variable (percent agreement and Cohen's κ)"; the single-coder fallback "delayed intra-rater re-code ... (limitation: no inter-rater reliability)"); OD-3 (`STEP2_OWNER_DECISIONS_RECORD.md` line 45: "No inter-rater reliability and no Cohen's κ may be claimed"); 12.21 row 6 (line 602), 12.24 decision 2 (DELEGATED), P-3 item 3, codebook revision 5 sections 5 and 5.3. State: `s7_recode.py` computes raw agreement only. Conflict (scope and verb, not a direct contradiction): see `CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_BRIEF.md` section 2. The sample (20 %, floor 20), the 14-day delay and `recode1_*` are silence-filling choices that the sources label delegated. I do not conclude that kappa will or will not be reported. Options: K1 raw only; K2 kappa with label; K3 kappa with threshold; K4 amend. Recommendation: K2 with a rule for undefined cases, values approved by the advisor. Impact: code only for K2/K3 (`agreement_table`, tests, codebook 5.1/5.3); codebook is an FZ item. Protocol change: K1/K2/K3 no (clarification of how the text is read; K1 departs from the literal line 1002), K4 yes. Acceptance: record entry stating the verb ("computed" / "reported" / "claimed") separately.

**D6 Coding eligibility (C-10).** Rule: spec §16 intro (line 925) "Only **parseable** responses (a `Verdict:` parsed under §13.4) are coded; UNP responses are counted and excluded"; codebook §1 line 32; §13.6.1 activity C (line 859) "redacted Comments of parseable responses". State: `s12_coding.py` codes non-SF calls with a parseable verdict; UNP and SF are counted (D_P, UNP, SF per family/condition), SF3 with a verdict is not coded. Conflict: "parseable" is silent on SF3 with a verdict, and on an SF call whose text is partial or absent (no Comments exist to code). Options: as implemented; SF3-with-verdict as a separate stratum; all with a parseable verdict. Recommendation: as implemented (consistent with SF-first and with the absence of a reliable Comments field, §16 line 925). Impact: D_P per condition reported (TH16, line 1161). Protocol change: no (clarification). Acceptance: coding population JSON lists UNP and SF counts beside D_P.

**D7 PRD-5 (C-11).** Rule: spec §11.2 step 3 (line 699) field-specific unit sets "PENDING RESEARCHER DECISION PRD-5"; §19.3 (line 1131). **Correction of a premise:** PRD-5 concerns the normalization of the human worksheet transcription in the blind check (§11.2), not the S2/S3 codebook; the codebook revision 5 has no unit-normalization rule (search: no match for the §11.2 procedure; line 71 concerns relation symbols). State: `step9/s9_blindcheck.py` `UNITS` and `normalize` implement the printed sets. Conflict: printed rule versus "pending" flag. Options: adopt printed sets; older global list. Recommendation: adopt printed sets. Impact: a foreign unit gives CHECKER ERROR (transcription), logged, member kept; no effect on registry or primary analysis. Protocol change: no. Acceptance: record entry; evaluation of the blind check only after it.

**Q-P1 returned model (C-12).** Rule: FZ-v1 row 17 "returned_model_id: recorded for every call"; spec §17 row 17 (line 1036) "record the returned model ID"; no consequence stated. Precedent only from the pilot study: `pilot_clarification/stage2_analyzer_specification.md` line 45 (I10: "reported, never blocking ... flagged MODEL_ID_DIFFERS ... no effect on counts"); in both pilot-study datasets the returned ID equalled the requested one exactly (Stage 1 manifest `models_returned`, Stage 2 `postrun.json`). State: recorded; integrity reports exact differences; no stop. Conflict: a legitimate alias or version suffix cannot be distinguished from a different model without a live observation (UNRESOLVED); the record's planning table says the returned ID is required "because deprecated IDs can be routed to another model" (record line 68, status "OWNER DECISION (planning) — NOT FROZEN"). Options: record only; flag in the pilot report; safe stop for owner review; automatic stop. Recommendation: record and flag always; stop for owner review on any returned ID that is missing or not exactly the requested one, and let the owner decide in writing, after the first live observation, which alias forms count as the same model. Protocol change: record/flag no; automatic stop yes. Acceptance: a mismatch cannot be silently included in a pilot decision.

**Q-P2 unlisted finish reason (C-13).** Rule: silent (spec §13.4 knows only the token limit and "safety/blocked"). State: such a response is scored by content and the reason is counted (`unlisted_finish_reasons`). Options: parse decides; structural failure; stop for owner review. Recommendation: stop for owner review before the next call (the response stays recorded, nothing is replaced), because the reason may mean an unusable answer. Protocol change: no (a safety stop is not a classification); automatic SF classification would be a new rule. Acceptance: a run that meets an unlisted reason ends marked INCOMPLETE with the reason counted, not scored as valid.

**Q-P3 blocked responses (C-14).** Rule: §13.4 SF4 "safety/blocked finish reason or no candidate returned". State: `BLOCK_REASONS` = SAFETY, BLOCKLIST, PROHIBITED_CONTENT, SPII, IMAGE_SAFETY, RECITATION, OTHER, LANGUAGE; plus a stored `block_reason` and "200 with no candidate and no text" ⇒ SF4. Source basis: none of the names is documented in the repository (the FZ-v1 `model_verification` lists no finish-reason vocabulary); they come from the assistant's memory of the public enum and are UNVERIFIED. Conflict: RECITATION, OTHER and LANGUAGE are not shown to mean "blocked". Options: keep all eight; five explicit block names only; only the structural signals (no candidate; non-empty block reason). Recommendation: classify as SF4 only the structural signals (no candidate, a block reason field) and names whose block meaning is documented by the provider once seen live; treat every other non-STOP, non-token-limit name as unlisted (Q-P2). Protocol change: no. Acceptance: tests per name after the first live observation.

**Q-P4 HTTP/error records (C-15).** Rule: §13.4 SF1 "HTTP/API error or timeout". State (12.36): error marker or status outside 2xx ⇒ SF1 even with text; no status and no error ⇒ not classified, counted as `records_without_http_status`. Conflict: a record without status is incoherent but not an "HTTP error" by the text. Options: leave counted; SF1; stop for review. Recommendation: stop for review on the first such record (adapter always sets a status on a response; absence signals a harness fault), then fix or classify by written rule. Protocol change: no. Acceptance: a mock record without status ends the run INCOMPLETE and is never scored as success.

## 4. OD-10 and the input/cost limit (C-01)
Full text and options: `CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_BRIEF.md` section 1. **Added here.** Actual prompt sizes (from `step8_output/.../{main,pilot}/prompt_table.json`, field `chars`): main 789-1,016 characters (MIL at most 873, PAC at most 1,016); pilot 789-1,016. The Step 7 worst case was 878 (MIL) and 1,016 (PAC) with widest numerals, so the actual maximum is inside the accepted bound. Cost is a separate quantity: hard cap US$51.88 (2,496 slots) / US$52.63 (2,532) for 1,016 tokens assumed; one pilot round (18 calls) US$0.37; the limit of US$100 is reached only at about 63,381 input tokens per call; the dominant term is the output cap (8,192 tokens × US$2.50 per 1M). Not mixed: the **bound** answers "is the input size what we say"; the **ceiling** answers "can the cost exceed US$100".
- Locally guaranteed: the exact prompt bytes and their length (hashes, `prompt_table.json`); the output cap and retries in the settings check; the hard-cap cost as a function of the assumed input tokens; post-run observed usage (lower bound when incomplete).
- Needs the tokenizer/provider: the true number of input tokens (the assumption "a token covers at least one character" is untested; provider overhead tokens are unknown). Provider-side silent input truncation is not plausible for these sizes (the input limit recorded in FZ-v1 `model_verification` is 1,048,576 tokens), but UNRESOLVED without a live count. Output truncation is detected (finish reason at the token limit ⇒ SF3).
- Handling an input above the stated bound: today the runner does not compare prompt lengths with `input_tokens_per_call_assumed`; proposed gate check (IMPLEMENTATION PLAN): refuse if the maximum prompt length in characters exceeds the assumed tokens, unless the owner states a measured basis.
- Before a paid pilot: written basis in the authorization (O1/O3), prices re-read that day (PJR J-9), the hard-cap figure shown; before the main run: O2 or the O4 check on all 1,248 prompts. A conservative approach backed by evidence: assume tokens = characters (upper bound under the stated assumption), which keeps the cap at US$51.88; observation on a different model (2.91-3.10 characters per token) supports but does not prove the margin. Alternative if no measurement is possible: accept O1 explicitly. No provider token count was made. Protocol change: none (record entries), except O5 (changing the limit or model).

## 5. Timeout, transport halt and resume (C-16, C-17)
**CURRENT RULE.** FZ-v1 row 17: `timeout_s` 180, `delay_between_calls_s` 1, `concurrency` 1, `retries` 0; spec §13.8 (871-875): a main run halts at `c_stop` consecutive structural failures or when the cumulative share exceeds `theta_main` after `calls_min_main` calls; "A run interrupted by transport failure resumes only under the identical FZ version (plus any pure implementation fix under H1-H4), continuing at the next `call_index` of the same frozen order (no re-draw)"; failed calls are not re-run, completed calls not replaced. Code: `GeminiProvider` uses `urlopen(timeout=...)` (per socket operation; `TIMEOUT_SEMANTICS` in the run summary); the ledger writes `intent` before the call and `raw` after it; an existing output folder is refused.
**Local simulation (mock provider, temporary folder, 2026-10-13).** A `KeyboardInterrupt` raised by the provider at call 3: the run aborts; the ledger ends with `intent 3` and has no `raw 3`; 2 raw files exist; no status marker; `s10_integrity` reports "not exactly one status marker"; running again into the same folder is refused (`FileExistsError`). So an unresolved intent is detectable from the ledger (intent index without a raw entry), and the current code can neither resume nor reuse the folder.
**Answers (all RECOMMENDATION; the text does not decide them).**
- *Provable transport-only failure:* every call of the current consecutive-failure run has flags exactly `[SF1]` and no response text (derivable from the stored records); UNRESOLVED whether "transport-only" in §13.8 also requires an HTTP-level distinction (for example 429 versus 5xx versus timeout).
- *Unresolved intent:* an `intent` entry without a `raw` entry for the same index. It is "attempted, no record": the provider may have processed it. It must not be repeated automatically (a repeat would be a second call for one key; retries = 0); the owner records its disposition (reported, counted as unknown) before any resume.
- *Reset of halt counters:* carrying them over is the only reading that adds no new rule; a reset needs approval (protocol change).
- *Who authorizes:* the text names no authorizer (UNRESOLVED); proposed: the owner, as a recorded resume event with a new authorization for the remaining call count and the cost ceiling, under the identical FZ hash.
- *Preventing double calls:* resume appends to the same ledger/hash chain, starts at the first index without an `intent`, refuses if an unresolved intent exists without a recorded disposition, and refuses if the settings or the FZ hash differ.
- *Unclear provider status or rate limit:* stored as SF1 with the status code; retries stay 0; the owner decides when to resume.
- *Total timeout:* the text does not define the 180 s; an enforced total deadline (worker thread, deadline hit = SF1, never retried) changes which calls fail, so it is a behaviour change of a frozen setting; decide before the FZ hash (PROTOCOL if it changes outcomes). Recommendation: keep the per-operation behaviour, keep the new flag for calls above 180 s, decide a total deadline after the first live observation.
Local simulation tests to be written after adoption are in `MASTER_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md`. Do not call the provider.

## 6. FZ hash, P-2 and final freeze (C-03)
Dependency map, why a cycle appears and four designs: `CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_BRIEF.md` section 3. **Contract to decide** (RECOMMENDATION C built on A): *input* = canonical JSON {path → SHA-256} of every FZ item file, with the FZ-v1 register hash and the Step 7 and Step 8 manifest hashes as members; *excluded* = decision record, run folders, Step 10 harness and report tools (harness changes only by H1-H4), results, the future final freeze manifest; *timing* = computed once before the first pilot call and stored in a file whose bytes are hashed; *verification* = recompute from the files and compare with the value recorded in the decision record at step 11 and in every run manifest; *after freeze* = any change of an item changes the index, hence a new FZ version and a re-pilot (§13.6 versioning rule), except pure implementation fixes under H1-H4 which are logged and re-checked against the frozen regression; *enforcement* = **none today**: `s10_runner.py` mentions `PARAMETER_FREEZE_MANIFEST_FZ_v1.json` only to read the register and settings (verified: no `fz_hash`/index reference); `s7_decision.py` accepts any 64-hex string and stores "researcher-supplied, unverified" (lines 163-176). Enforcement would be CODE after adoption (gate check at the pilot and at the main run, and a verification inside the decision script). No format or code was changed.

## 7. Other open items (G)
PRD-6 anchor (C-18): spec §10 line 662 and §19.3 line 1132; FZ-v1 declares no store; `research_design/` is untracked; recommendation: the owner declares the store before the decision script runs; a commit is the owner's permission. PC-2 (C-20): spec line 1136; reading applied in 12.28; confirm. I-6 (C-21): spec §11 layer 1 (line 670), 12.23; informational (0 failures in the build report), a gate would change the fixed gate counts of §3.7 (PROTOCOL). P-3 (C-22): four items, three settled by owner entries, item 1 (`s2_values` "none" outside its denominator) open; P-4 (C-23): token adequacy, see section 4; P-5 (C-26): human readiness of the codebook (rehearsal pending); F-3 (C-24): technically satisfied by Step 8 taking values from the register (confirm to close); PRD-8 (C-25): not applicable under DP1/OD-4; round-1 FAIL-S action (C-27): §13.6 whitelist, researcher decision when it occurs; FZ-v1 manifest records the decision-record hash `94fa64f9...` while the record now differs, a documented historical mismatch (C-19); Step 8 design choices taken by the assistant under the owner's instruction (12.28: F-4 architecture, PG4 test, PG11a projection bundle, A6b actor) have no advisor review (C-28); price/model re-verification at the freeze, PJR J-9 (C-29); owner authorization content and the order pilot → decision → main (checklist). Live verification may be considered only when the criteria in `MASTER_PILOT_READINESS_CHECKLIST.md` section 4 are met.

